WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:08.820
The peak–end rule is a psychological phenomenon&nbsp;
that occurs when people judge an experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:14.400
largely on how they felt at its peak and&nbsp;
its end. There are two reasons for this.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.120
More emotionally intense events&nbsp;
lead to better memories than less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.520
intense events. So if you experience a&nbsp;
drama, the peak is what’s most moving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.640
and to the mind that’s the&nbsp;
most vivid mental snapshot.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.060
We have a better memory of the&nbsp;
end of a sequence — a phenomena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.280
known as recency bias. If you get to&nbsp;
eat 3 different desserts in a row,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.020
the second dessert forms a new memory that&nbsp;
makes it harder for you to recall the first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:51.660
And when you’ve finished all three, your&nbsp;
brain remembers the last one most clearly.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.840
However, the rule does not always work.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:04.140
As we go into an experience, our expectations&nbsp;
play a lead role. If they are initially not met,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.280
our prediction error can affect our&nbsp;
perception of what’s about to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.340
next — including the peak. But for now, let’s&nbsp;
assume that everything goes according to plan.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:21.900
Say you go eat out at a cheap diner,&nbsp;
and your expectations are low. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.000
the memory of your evening will be formed&nbsp;
by the peak and the end. The dry salad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.460
the bad music, and the dirty bathroom&nbsp;
are long forgotten. Later you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.680
rate the experience as exceptional. But&nbsp;
what if your expectations are not met?

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.260
If you go out to eat at an expensive restaurant,&nbsp;
you can have high expectations. If the initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.420
experience doesn't meet them, your mind decides&nbsp;
that the place isn’t that good after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:57.120
and you’ll see everything through an overly&nbsp;
critical lens. The peak is discounted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.460
the end outrageous, and then the&nbsp;
overall experience is rated poorly.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.360
So if expectations are low, the&nbsp;
rule works. If they are high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.120
it's important that they&nbsp;
are met right at the start.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:18.060
Evidence for the rule was established&nbsp;
through an experiment in which people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.140
were subjected to an unpleasant&nbsp;
experience in two different ways.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:37.690
for the same amount of time, but then&nbsp;
kept it there for another 30 seconds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:43.810
during which the temperature was raised by&nbsp;
about 1° celsius. The subjects were then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:48.610
offered the option of which of the two&nbsp;
trials to repeat. And as it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:54.550
they were more willing to repeat the second trial,&nbsp;
despite the prolonged exposure to the cold water.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.610
Kahneman, Fredrickson, Schreiber, and Redelmeier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:05.530
who conducted the study in 1993,&nbsp;
concluded that "people chose the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.730
long trial simply because they liked the&nbsp;
memory of it better than the alternative”.

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:17.050
If you happen to run a restaurant and&nbsp;
manage to meet your customers expectations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:22.930
you can create a wonderful peak, finish&nbsp;
with a fantastic end and deliver an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:27.310
experience that’s not only excellent,&nbsp;
but will also be remembered as such.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:36.790
Now what do you think about the rule? And did you&nbsp;
